WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.620
-: Alright, welcome back everyone.

00:01.620 --> 00:04.290
Next up we have a section that I'm going to say is

00:04.290 --> 00:07.200
the least important for the entire course.

00:07.200 --> 00:09.180
That doesn't mean that I don't like it.

00:09.180 --> 00:11.400
I actually think it's very interesting,

00:11.400 --> 00:14.310
fascinating behind the scenes peak at how Git works.

00:14.310 --> 00:16.320
But you really don't have to understand any

00:16.320 --> 00:18.450
of this in order to use Git.

00:18.450 --> 00:20.400
So far we haven't really talked about it

00:20.400 --> 00:22.410
we haven't understood it,

00:22.410 --> 00:24.210
but we've been using Git just fine.

00:24.210 --> 00:26.610
So a lot of Git users won't go into any

00:26.610 --> 00:29.310
of this or they won't be bothered to understand things

00:29.310 --> 00:31.020
like Git objects and blobs

00:31.020 --> 00:34.140
and trees and how Git hashes things.

00:34.140 --> 00:38.070
And you know, it's important to understand

00:38.070 --> 00:41.580
to make you a more well-rounded Git developer, I guess.

00:41.580 --> 00:44.550
But it's not critical to actually using it.

00:44.550 --> 00:48.000
Anyway, let's start by highlighting probably

00:48.000 --> 00:51.690
the one thing that I would say is slightly more important.

00:51.690 --> 00:53.400
At the beginning of this section, we're going to talk

00:53.400 --> 00:56.033
about the contents of the .Git directory

00:56.033 --> 00:59.250
and just take a look at some of the more important pieces.

00:59.250 --> 01:01.980
So I'll show you the local config file.

01:01.980 --> 01:03.420
Eh, it will come up probably for you

01:03.420 --> 01:06.060
at some point if you continue using Git

01:06.060 --> 01:09.180
and it has more real world applications, I guess

01:09.180 --> 01:11.310
than some of the more conceptual things.

01:11.310 --> 01:12.870
So everything else, eh

01:12.870 --> 01:15.300
understanding the refs directory, not that important.

01:15.300 --> 01:16.590
Understanding the head file

01:16.590 --> 01:19.080
we've already seen it actually very early on

01:19.080 --> 01:20.942
when I first introduced branches.

01:20.942 --> 01:23.280
Then when I talk about hashing functions

01:23.280 --> 01:27.690
and Git objects, Git trees, Git blobs, all of that stuff.

01:27.690 --> 01:29.190
And I'll show you some commands

01:29.190 --> 01:30.990
some new Git commands that are,

01:30.990 --> 01:32.520
they're called plumbing commands.

01:32.520 --> 01:34.770
Therefore, behind the scenes usages

01:34.770 --> 01:37.920
they're not really consumer facing commands as much

01:37.920 --> 01:40.590
as you could say any command and Git is consumer facing.

01:40.590 --> 01:42.690
But anyway, that's all encompassed

01:42.690 --> 01:44.220
in these two at the bottom.

01:44.220 --> 01:45.960
So all of that is nice to have.

01:45.960 --> 01:47.700
It's great if you watch these videos

01:47.700 --> 01:49.620
if you make it through them, if you find them interesting.

01:49.620 --> 01:51.630
But if you struggle to understand some

01:51.630 --> 01:52.463
of what I'm talking about,

01:52.463 --> 01:55.230
if I do a poor job of teaching you these concepts,

01:55.230 --> 01:57.900
you will still be a perfectly fine Git developer.

01:57.900 --> 02:00.330
But let's hope that I don't do a poor job.

02:00.330 --> 02:01.163
Okay.
